notes.body:
A local terraform.tfstate works for solo learning but fails teams: it cannot be shared, it is lost if your laptop dies, and two engineers can apply concurrently and corrupt it. A remote backend stores state in shared storage and adds locking. For Azure, the azurerm backend keeps state in a Blob container and takes a blob lease as the lock — any second terraform apply fails fast with a lock error instead of corrupting state. Worked example backend block:

terraform {
  backend "azurerm" {
    resource_group_name  = "rg-terraform-state"
    storage_account_name = "sttfstateprod01"
    container_name       = "tfstate"
    key                  = "prod.terraform.tfstate"
  }
}

After adding this, run terraform init -migrate-state to move the local state into the blob. Sensitive values live in state in plain JSON — that is why the storage account must restrict access and why state files are never committed to git. Interview answer: remote state plus locking is what makes terraform apply safe with more than one operator; the lock error is a feature, not a bug, and you never force-unlock without confirming no apply is actually running.

