module "team_storage" {
  source       = "./modules/storage"
  account_name = "stx01"
  containers = {
    logs    = "private"
    reports = "blob"
  }
}

output "primary_blob_endpoint" {
  value = azurerm_storage_account.this.primary_blob_endpoint
}

variable "retention_days" {
  type    = number
  default = 30
  validation {
    condition     = var.retention_days >= 1 && var.retention_days <= 365
    error_message = "retention_days must be between 1 and 365."
  }
}

output "container_ids" {
  value = { for k, c in azurerm_storage_container.team : k => c.id }
}

notes.body:
The panel's follow-up: requirements change — each container needs its own access type (private, blob, or container). A set of names can no longer express this; you refactor to a map of name to access type. Worked diff, before and after. Before:

  for_each              = toset(["logs", "reports", "uploads"])
  name                  = each.key
  container_access_type = "private"

After:

variable "containers" {
  type = map(string)
  default = {
    logs    = "private"
    reports = "blob"
    uploads = "container"
  }
}

resource "azurerm_storage_container" "team" {
  for_each              = var.containers
  name                  = each.key
  storage_account_name  = azurerm_storage_account.data.name
  container_access_type = each.value
}

Because the instance keys ("logs", "reports", "uploads") are unchanged, terraform plan shows only in-place updates to container_access_type — zero destroys, zero recreates. That is the sentence the panel wants: 'The map keys preserve identity, so the change is surgical.' Had the original used count, the same requirement change would have been a rewrite with recreation risk.

A module is a directory of .tf files with its own variables and outputs, called via a module block. It turns the storage-account pattern into a reusable unit the next team consumes in five lines. Worked example — modules/storage/main.tf:

variable "account_name" { type = string }
variable "resource_group_name" { type = string }
variable "location" { type = string }
variable "containers" { type = map(string) }

resource "azurerm_storage_account" "this" {
  name                     = var.account_name
  resource_group_name      = var.resource_group_name
  location                 = var.location
  account_tier             = "Standard"
  account_replication_type = "LRS"
}

resource "azurerm_storage_container" "team" {
  for_each              = var.containers
  name                  = each.key
  storage_account_name  = azurerm_storage_account.this.name
  container_access_type = each.value
}

output "primary_blob_endpoint" {
  value = azurerm_storage_account.this.primary_blob_endpoint
}

Root call: module "team_storage" { source = "./modules/storage"; account_name = "stteamdata01"; resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.main.name; location = "eastus"; containers = { logs = "private", reports = "blob" } }. Inside a module, resources address as module.team_storage.azurerm_storage_account.this. Interview line: modules are how you stop copy-pasting the same storage pattern into twelve repos.

Variables are module inputs; outputs are module return values. Every variable gets a type and, where it matters, a validation block that fails fast with a clear message instead of a cryptic provider error twenty minutes into an apply. Worked example:

variable "environment" {
  description = "Deployment environment"
  type        = string
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "stage", "qa", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be one of: dev, stage, qa, prod."
  }
}

variable "allowed_ips" {
  description = "CIDR blocks allowed to reach the storage account"
  type        = list(string)
  default     = []
}

Outputs expose what callers and humans need: output "storage_account_id" { value = azurerm_storage_account.this.id }. Two discipline rules: mark secrets with sensitive = true so they are redacted from console output (they still land in state — sensitive is display-only, not encryption), and never give a variable a default that is only valid in one environment; force the caller to choose. Predict-the-plan drill: changing a variable default with no other edits produces no plan — defaults are inputs, and identical inputs mean identical infrastructure.

In Jenkins, credentials live in the credential store (never in the Jenkinsfile), and the credentials binding plugin injects them as masked environment variables for the duration of a step. Worked example — declarative pipeline:

pipeline {
  agent any
  stages {
    stage('Terraform Plan') {
      steps {
        withCredentials([
          string(credentialsId: 'ARM_CLIENT_SECRET', variable: 'ARM_CLIENT_SECRET'),
          string(credentialsId: 'TF_VAR_db_password', variable: 'TF_VAR_db_password')
        ]) {
          sh '''
            terraform init -input=false
            terraform plan -var-file=environments/prod.tfvars -out=tfplan
          '''
        }
      }
    }
    stage('Apply (manual gate)') {
      input { message 'Apply this plan to prod?' }
      steps {
        withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'ARM_CLIENT_SECRET', variable: 'ARM_CLIENT_SECRET')]) {
          sh 'terraform apply -input=false tfplan'
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Key points for the panel: credentials are referenced by ID, Jenkins masks them in console output, the binding only exists inside the withCredentials block, and the plan file built in the earlier stage is what the gated apply consumes — the human approves a known plan, not a re-plan.

Vault is a secrets broker: instead of a long-lived password sitting in CI, Terraform (or the pipeline) authenticates to Vault and receives a short-lived, automatically revoked credential. Concept-level flow, which is all the panel needs: the Jenkins agent authenticates to Vault with its own identity (e.g., a Vault AppRole or the cloud IAM identity), requests database credentials from the database secrets engine, and gets a username/password pair with a 1-hour TTL. Terraform consumes it via the Vault provider's data source:

data "vault_kv_secret_v2" "db" {
  mount = "kv"
  name  = "app/database"
}

and references data.vault_kv_secret_v2.db.data["password"]. When the TTL expires, Vault revokes the credential — a leaked value dies on its own. Contrast with the env-var pattern: env vars are simpler and fine for many teams, but the secret is static until someone rotates it; Vault adds rotation and audit (every read is logged with an identity). Interview line: 'Env vars for static secrets with disciplined rotation; Vault when you need dynamic, short-lived credentials with an audit trail.'

Consul answers 'where is the thing I need to connect to' without hardcoding addresses in Terraform. Two concepts. Service discovery: services register themselves (name, address, port, health checks); consumers query the catalog instead of baking IPs into config. Key/value config: hierarchical config (app/config/db_host) that Terraform can read at plan time. Worked example — Terraform reading a Consul KV entry to avoid hardcoding:

data "consul_keys" "app" {
  key {
    name = "db_host"
    path = "app/config/db_host"
  }
}

resource "azurerm_storage_account" "this" {
  name = "st${data.consul_keys.app.var.db_host}01"  # illustrative only
  ...
}

The real-world shape is simpler than the syntax: the database team publishes the host in Consul once; every Terraform config reads it instead of copying a hostname into twelve repos. Interview line: 'Consul is how config stops being copy-pasted — Terraform reads the current value at plan time instead of trusting a stale tfvars entry.' Keep it at concept level: know what problems it solves (discovery, shared config), not its full API.

Removal exposes the model difference completely. In Terraform, infrastructure exists because config declares it, so removal is config-driven: delete the resource block from the .tf file and run terraform apply — the plan shows '1 to destroy' and apply deletes the real object. Two variants: terraform destroy -target=azurerm_virtual_machine.old removes one resource as a one-off without editing files (useful in emergencies, but the config still declares it, so the next full apply would recreate it — you must still remove the block). And the removed block (Terraform 1.7+) de-adopts without destroying:

removed {
  from = azurerm_virtual_machine.old
  lifecycle {
    destroy = false
  }
}

— apply drops it from state but leaves the real VM running, for objects another team now owns. In Ansible there is no state and no plan: you write a task with state: absent (e.g., azure_rm_virtualmachine with state: absent, or apt with state: absent) and run the playbook — deletion is just another task you authored. Nothing warns you that the task will delete; there is no destroy plan to review. Interview line: 'Terraform removal is a reviewed plan against tracked state; Ansible removal is a task you wrote and ran — powerful, but with no safety net.'
